{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Gerken2003-double-chirped-60layer-f0p33",
  "Source": "M. Gerken and D. A. B. Miller, \"Multilayer thin-film structures with high spatial dispersion,\" Appl. Opt. 42, 1330-1345 (2003). doi:10.1364/AO.42.001330 - Sec. 4, Eqs. (9)-(14) with f = 0.33, p = 1..30, P_DC = 25 (text p. 1335); Fig. 5",
  "Notes": "Double-chirped SiO2/Ta2O5 mirror giving an approximately linear spatial shift with wavelength (wavelength-dependent penetration depth)\nSpecification: Approximately linear spatial shift with wavelength at 45 deg, p polarization; impedance-matched (no front reflection); maximum shift roughly constant (~10-12 um) for the allowed ripple\nReported: Fig. 5(b) (read from plot): shift rises approximately linearly from 0 near 830-840 nm to ~10-12 um; the f = 0 design (circles) reaches ~12 um by ~870 nm, larger f values spread the shift to longer wavelengths (f = 0.5 up to ~1060 nm).\nNotes: THICKNESSES ARE COMPUTED here from the paper's Eqs. (10)-(14), not tabulated in the paper. Assumptions: n_H = 2.06 (Ta2O5), n_L = 1.456 (SiO2) from Sec. 2; theta = 45 deg in vacuum; period p = 1 next to the incident medium (impedance-matching chirp faces the incoming light, consistent with Fig. 6); within each period Ta2O5 is placed first (order within a period is not stated); d_LDC = [lambda_B(p)/2 - d_HDC n_H cos_H] / (n_L cos_L), i.e. Eq. (14) read so that each period keeps its Bragg wavelength per Eq. (9). Incident medium (quartz n = 1.52 assumed, as in the experiments) and exit medium (air) are NOT stated for these calculated designs; no back Bragg reflector is included (the paper says 60-layer structures).\nLayers from Sec. 4, Eqs. (9)-(14) with f = 0.33, p = 1..30, P_DC = 25 (text p. 1335); Fig. 5; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
